WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.160
Propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol share the formula C three

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.200
H eight O, so in a mass spectrum

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.760
both molecular ions sit at 60.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.440
Both are alcohols, so both infrared spectra show

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.120
the broad O H band.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:17.360
So how do you tell them apart?

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:20.080
Run a carbon-13 NMR spectrum.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.640
One gives three peaks, and the other gives

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:23.200
two.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.360
By the end of this video, you'll know

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.120
which is which, and why.

00:00:28.334 --> 00:00:32.053
NMR stands for nuclear magnetic resonance.

00:00:32.334 --> 00:00:36.813
Some nuclei, like hydrogen-1 and carbon-13, behave like

00:00:36.894 --> 00:00:38.413
tiny bar magnets.

00:00:38.654 --> 00:00:41.773
Ordinary carbon-12 doesn't, so the machine can't see

00:00:41.854 --> 00:00:42.013
it.

00:00:42.414 --> 00:00:44.813
Put the sample in a very strong magnet,

00:00:44.854 --> 00:00:47.293
and these tiny magnets line up either with

00:00:47.374 --> 00:00:48.813
the field or against it.

00:00:49.214 --> 00:00:51.133
Lining up against it takes a little more

00:00:51.214 --> 00:00:51.853
energy.

00:00:52.094 --> 00:00:54.253
A pulse of radio waves at exactly the

00:00:54.334 --> 00:00:57.293
right frequency flips them, and that absorption is

00:00:57.334 --> 00:00:59.293
the resonance the machine detects.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:01.780
Here's the useful part.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:05.620
Every nucleus sits inside a cloud of electrons,

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.620
and those electrons shield it a little from

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:08.500
the big magnet.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.300
Now bond that carbon to an oxygen.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:15.540
Oxygen is electronegative, so it pulls electron density

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:18.020
away, and the carbon feels more of the

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:18.580
field.

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:20.820
We say it's deshielded.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:24.020
It needs a slightly higher frequency to flip,

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.420
so its peak lands in a different place.

00:01:27.634 --> 00:01:30.593
That position is the chemical shift, measured in

00:01:30.754 --> 00:01:32.913
parts per million, or ppm.

00:01:33.554 --> 00:01:36.433
The scale runs backwards, from right to left.

00:01:37.074 --> 00:01:39.553
Zero is set by a reference compound called

00:01:39.714 --> 00:01:42.353
TMS, which is added to the sample, and

00:01:42.434 --> 00:01:43.873
its peak isn't counted.

00:01:44.514 --> 00:01:47.073
The more deshielded a carbon is, the further

00:01:47.234 --> 00:01:48.513
left its peak sits.

00:01:49.074 --> 00:01:52.673
For carbon-13, nearly every peak lands between 0

00:01:52.754 --> 00:01:55.073
and about 220 ppm.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:58.460
So what makes two carbons give the same

00:01:58.540 --> 00:01:59.180
peak?

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:02.780
They need identical surroundings, which chemists call the

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:03.980
same environment.

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:06.860
In ethanol, one carbon is bonded to the

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:08.620
oxygen and the other isn't.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:11.900
They're in different environments, so the spectrum shows

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:13.060
two peaks.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.300
The carbon next to the oxygen is deshielded,

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:19.580
so it sits further left, near 58 ppm.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:21.820
The other sits near 18.

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:25.020
That's the key rule: count the peaks, and

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:27.100
you've counted the carbon environments.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:29.660
Back to the propanols.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.940
In propan-1-ol, the O H sits on the

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:34.140
end carbon.

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:36.300
Each of the three carbons has a different

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:38.780
set of neighbours, so you get three peaks.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:42.140
In propan-2-ol, the O H sits on the

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:43.020
middle carbon.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:45.100
Draw a mirror line through it, and the

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.180
two end carbons swap places.

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:51.660
Carbons that swap share one environment, so propan-2-ol

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:53.180
gives just two peaks.

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:55.500
Same formula, different count.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.300
Try the swap test on two ketones.

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.380
Butan-2-one has its C equals O on the

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:03.420
second carbon.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:05.740
Walk in from each end and the routes

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:08.940
differ, so all four carbons are different, giving

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:09.820
four peaks.

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:13.980
Pentan-3-one has five carbons, but its C equals

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:15.500
O sits right in the middle.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.060
The two sides match, so it gives only

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:19.260
three peaks.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:22.300
Counting carbon atoms isn't the same as counting

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:23.420
environments.

00:03:24.634 --> 00:03:26.713
You might expect a taller peak to mean

00:03:26.794 --> 00:03:27.673
more carbons.

00:03:28.154 --> 00:03:28.993
Not here.

00:03:29.274 --> 00:03:31.753
Only about one carbon atom in a hundred

00:03:31.834 --> 00:03:34.873
is carbon-13, so two of them are rarely

00:03:34.954 --> 00:03:35.833
bonded together.

00:03:36.474 --> 00:03:38.713
The machine is also run so that neighbouring

00:03:38.794 --> 00:03:41.833
hydrogens can't split the carbon signals, which is

00:03:41.954 --> 00:03:42.873
called decoupling.

00:03:43.354 --> 00:03:46.393
So each environment shows as one sharp line.

00:03:47.114 --> 00:03:50.473
Peak heights in carbon-13 aren't reliable counts, so

00:03:50.634 --> 00:03:52.153
don't read them as ratios.

00:03:52.634 --> 00:03:55.033
Count the lines, and read where they sit.

00:03:56.234 --> 00:03:58.473
Where a peak sits tells you what kind

00:03:58.554 --> 00:03:59.593
of carbon it is.

00:04:00.154 --> 00:04:02.313
You don't need to memorise the ranges, because

00:04:02.354 --> 00:04:03.993
they're printed on the data sheet.

00:04:04.314 --> 00:04:07.593
Take ethyl ethanoate, an ester with four carbon

00:04:07.674 --> 00:04:08.393
environments.

00:04:08.954 --> 00:04:11.913
The peak at 171 falls in the ester

00:04:11.994 --> 00:04:16.553
and acid range, 160 to 185, so it's

00:04:16.634 --> 00:04:18.393
the C equals O carbon.

00:04:18.954 --> 00:04:20.953
The peak at 60 falls in the C

00:04:21.034 --> 00:04:23.913
O range, 50 to 90, so it's the

00:04:23.994 --> 00:04:26.073
carbon bonded to the ester oxygen.

00:04:26.634 --> 00:04:29.353
The peaks at 21 and 14 are alkyl

00:04:29.434 --> 00:04:32.313
carbons, further from the oxygens, on the right.

00:04:33.534 --> 00:04:36.493
The ranges overlap, so one shift can fit

00:04:36.574 --> 00:04:38.653
more than one kind of carbon.

00:04:38.734 --> 00:04:41.613
A peak at 21 fits plain alkyl, and

00:04:41.694 --> 00:04:43.453
it also fits a carbon next to a

00:04:43.534 --> 00:04:45.053
C equals O.

00:04:45.134 --> 00:04:47.053
A peak at 70 could be a carbon

00:04:47.134 --> 00:04:50.093
bonded to oxygen, or one bonded to chlorine

00:04:50.134 --> 00:04:51.293
or bromine.

00:04:51.454 --> 00:04:52.893
So check the formula.

00:04:52.974 --> 00:04:55.133
If there's no chlorine or bromine in it,

00:04:55.214 --> 00:04:58.173
the 70 points to a C O carbon.

00:04:58.254 --> 00:05:00.653
A shift narrows the choices, and the count

00:05:00.734 --> 00:05:02.493
and the formula finish the job.

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:05.180
Two lines to keep.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:08.300
Carbons that swap places share a peak.

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:11.380
And further left means more deshielded, so check

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:14.100
the data sheet to name each peak's group.

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:15.940
Your turn.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:18.660
The four alcohols on the card all have

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:21.900
the formula C four H ten O.

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:24.660
One of them gives a carbon-13 spectrum with

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:29.300
just two peaks, at 69 and 31 ppm.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:31.780
Which alcohol is it, and why?

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:33.140
Pause here to answer.

00:05:43.567 --> 00:05:45.886
It's 2-methylpropan-2-ol.

00:05:46.127 --> 00:05:48.606
Its three methyl groups all swap places around

00:05:48.647 --> 00:05:51.246
the central carbon, so they give one peak,

00:05:51.327 --> 00:05:52.606
at 31.

00:05:52.647 --> 00:05:55.086
The central carbon carries the O H, so

00:05:55.167 --> 00:05:57.006
it sits in the C O range, at

00:05:57.087 --> 00:05:58.286
69.

00:05:58.407 --> 00:06:03.326
Butan-1-ol and butan-2-ol give four peaks, and 2-methylpropan-1-ol

00:06:03.487 --> 00:06:04.286
gives three.

00:06:04.767 --> 00:06:07.566
Expecting four peaks from four carbons is the

00:06:07.647 --> 00:06:08.126
slip.

00:06:09.334 --> 00:06:09.973
Last one.

00:06:10.214 --> 00:06:13.173
A compound with the formula C three H

00:06:13.254 --> 00:06:16.453
six O two is either propanoic acid or

00:06:16.614 --> 00:06:17.733
methyl ethanoate.

00:06:18.214 --> 00:06:22.693
Its carbon-13 spectrum has peaks at 171, 52

00:06:23.174 --> 00:06:24.933
and 21 ppm.

00:06:25.494 --> 00:06:28.053
Identify the compound, and use the data sheet

00:06:28.134 --> 00:06:29.413
to justify your answer.

00:06:29.974 --> 00:06:31.253
Pause here to answer.

00:06:41.667 --> 00:06:43.386
It's methyl ethanoate.

00:06:43.667 --> 00:06:46.626
The peak at 171 is a C equals

00:06:46.787 --> 00:06:50.866
O carbon, but the 160 to 185 range

00:06:50.947 --> 00:06:54.306
covers acids and esters, so it can't decide.

00:06:54.867 --> 00:06:56.706
The peak at 52 does.

00:06:57.187 --> 00:06:59.426
It sits in the C O range, so

00:06:59.587 --> 00:07:01.586
one carbon is joined to an oxygen by

00:07:01.667 --> 00:07:04.306
a single bond, and that's the ester's methyl

00:07:04.387 --> 00:07:04.786
group.

00:07:05.267 --> 00:07:07.826
Propanoic acid has no peak in that range.

00:07:08.227 --> 00:07:11.426
Calling 171 an acid is the trap.

00:07:12.634 --> 00:07:16.073
So, with a carbon-13 spectrum, count the peaks

00:07:16.154 --> 00:07:19.033
to count the carbon environments, and use symmetry

00:07:19.114 --> 00:07:21.113
to merge carbons that swap.

00:07:21.194 --> 00:07:23.433
Then read each shift off the data sheet,

00:07:23.474 --> 00:07:26.313
and let the formula settle any overlap.

00:07:26.394 --> 00:07:30.153
Next, proton NMR, where the peaks carry even

00:07:30.314 --> 00:07:33.113
more: how many hydrogens there are, and who

00:07:33.234 --> 00:07:34.153
their neighbours are.

00:07:35.367 --> 00:07:36.806
Here are the key notes.

00:07:37.047 --> 00:07:38.966
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:39.047 --> 00:07:39.846
to copy them.
